    {"problem": "Your side yard is barely a metre wide", "solution": "A standard condenser pad will not fit, and the neighbour's wall is right there. We use a wall bracket to lift the outdoor unit off the ground, or we place it in the rear yard or on a flat roof where the structure and your building rules allow. The line set gets routed along the brick in a cover, not across a window."},
    {"problem": "The house has radiators and no ducts at all", "solution": "Plenty of Annex and Riverdale homes never had a furnace. A multi-zone ductless system puts a quiet wall head in each main room with one outdoor unit, or a slim ducted air handler in the attic or basement feeds short duct runs. Either way, you keep the radiators as backup and gain cooling you never had."},
    {"problem": "Baseboard heat in a condo townhouse and a Toronto Hydro bill that stings", "solution": "Electric baseboards give you one unit of heat per unit of hydro. A cold-climate heat pump gives you two to three in mild weather, and because the home is heated by electricity it sits in the top rebate tier, up to $7,500. We also help with the placement request your property manager will want."},
    {"problem": "The furnace is 20 years old and the AC died last August", "solution": "One cold-climate heat pump replaces both. If you are on Enbridge gas and want the cheapest possible backup for the deepest cold, we keep or replace the furnace and run the two as a hybrid. If you would rather leave gas behind, an electric air handler with heat strips covers the rest."},
    {"problem": "1950s ductwork can't move the air a heat pump needs", "solution": "Heat pumps deliver warm air at a lower temperature than a furnace, so they need more airflow to carry the same heat. Undersized returns and crushed flex duct in a Scarborough or Etobicoke bungalow choke that. We measure static pressure, add or enlarge returns and seal joints so the system you pay for performs."},
    {"problem": "Your 100 amp panel is already full", "solution": "Our ESA-licensed electricians run a load calculation first. Many Toronto homes can take a 240V heat pump circuit on the existing service once we tidy up the panel. When they cannot, a panel upgrade goes into the written quote, not onto a surprise invoice."},
