  {"type": "split", "h2": "How we size a heat pump for a Toronto semi or bungalow", "photo": "sys",
   "html": "<p>Old Toronto brick loses heat through solid walls and thin attic insulation, so a 1,400 square foot semi can need more heating capacity than a newer 2,000 square foot house in Markham. That is why we never size from square footage. We measure the envelope and run the numbers.</p><p>Then we find the balance point. That is the outdoor temperature where the heat pump's output meets the house's heat loss. Below it, backup heat helps out. On Enbridge gas, that backup is usually your furnace in a <a href='/heat-pump/hybrid-furnace-systems/'>hybrid setup</a>. On Toronto Hydro alone, it is electric strips in an air handler that run only on the coldest nights and during defrost.</p><p>Oversizing is the most common mistake we see on Toronto installs. A unit that is too big short cycles, leaves the house clammy in July and wears out early. An inverter heat pump sized right runs long and low, which is exactly what a draughty old house wants.</p><ul class=\"checklist\"><li>Heat loss measured room by room, not estimated</li><li>Capacity checked at -15°C, not just the nameplate</li><li>Backup heat sized to the balance point for your fuel</li><li>Ductwork tested so the airflow matches the unit</li></ul><p>Want the full explanation of how these units hold capacity in deep cold? Read our page on <a href='/heat-pump/cold-climate/'>cold-climate heat pumps across Ontario</a>, or see everything we do for <a href='/toronto/'>HVAC in Toronto</a>.</p>"},
